<!--
This template is a modular checklist, not a mandatory outline. Keep only sections
that materially improve the note. Delete unused headings and placeholders, and
avoid repeating an explanation under several headings. Publication-ready notes
generated by the scheduled task should set `draft: false`.

Choose modules according to the topic:

- Probability concept: definitions, equations, properties, relationships, toy example.
- Distribution: support, PMF/PDF, parameters, moments, relationships, simulation.
- Asymptotic result: conditions, theorem, intuition, derivation outline, failure cases, simulation.
- Estimator or test: estimand, statistic, sampling distribution, uncertainty, interpretation.
- Regression model: data-generating model, coefficients, estimation, assumptions, diagnostics.
- Bayesian method: prior, likelihood, posterior, computation, posterior predictive checks.
- Applied bioinformatics method: experimental unit, design, normalization, implementation, diagnostics.
- Conceptual warning: failure mechanism, examples, remedies, and sensitivity analysis.

All placeholder executable cells must use `#| eval: false`. Enable evaluation only
after replacing placeholders with reproducible code that works in the site environment.
-->

# Topic at a Glance

> **One-sentence summary:** Explain the topic in language a researcher from another field can understand.

| Item | Description |
|---|---|
| **Statistical domain** | Probability / Inference / Regression / Bayesian / Survival / etc. |
| **Main question** | The scientific or statistical question this topic addresses |
| **Data or variable type** | Continuous / Binary / Count / Categorical / Time-to-event / etc. |
| **Experimental unit** | The independent unit on which inference is based, when applicable |
| **Unit of replication** | Biological or technical replicate, when applicable |
| **Target population** | Population to which the conclusion is intended to apply |
| **Parameter or estimand** | Unknown target quantity, when applicable |
| **Typical applications** | Concrete uses in bioinformatics or biology |
| **Prerequisites** | Concepts readers should understand first |
| **Related topics** | Links to neighboring notes in the knowledge map |

# Why This Matters

Start with a concrete scientific or biological question. Explain why randomness,
sampling, measurement, limited sample size, heterogeneity, or confounding makes
the question statistically difficult.

Identify the relevant question type rather than discussing every type:

- **Descriptive:** What was observed?
- **Estimation:** What population quantity should be learned?
- **Association:** How are variables related?
- **Causal:** What would change under an intervention?
- **Prediction:** How accurately can an unseen outcome be predicted?

> Association, prediction, and causation are distinct goals and require different assumptions.

# Intuition

Explain the topic without equations first. Use a small biological example or a
clear mental model, then state the central insight.

> **Key intuition:** ...

# Definitions and Notation

Define only the terms and symbols used later. Distinguish where relevant:

- population, sample, and target population;
- random variable and observed value;
- fixed known quantity and unknown parameter;
- parameter, estimand, estimator, and estimate;
- biological replicate and technical replicate.

| Symbol or term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| $Y$ | Outcome or response |
| $X$ | Predictor or covariate |
| $\theta$ | Unknown parameter |
| $\hat{\theta}$ | Estimator or observed estimate |

# Core Statistical Idea

Present the central model, theorem, estimator, test, or reasoning pattern. Explain
why it is appropriate for the scientific question and data-generating process.

```text
Scientific question
        ↓
Data-generating assumptions
        ↓
Statistical model or principle
        ↓
Estimate, prediction, or decision
```

## Data-generating process

<!-- Keep when understanding the source of variation matters. -->

```text
Biological system
    ↓
Biological variation and sampling
    ↓
Measurement process
    ↓
Observed data
```

## Key equation

<!-- Keep when an equation materially improves understanding. -->

$$
\boxed{\text{Insert the key equation or model here}}
$$

Explain every important variable and the intuition behind the equation.

# Probability Model

<!-- Optional: especially useful for distributions and probabilistic models. -->

State the support and parameters before giving the PMF or PDF.

$$
p(x\mid\theta)=\ldots
$$

Give relevant moments and relationships:

$$
\operatorname{E}[X]=\ldots,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\ldots
$$

Explain limiting cases, conjugate relationships, or connections to other
distributions only when useful.

# Derivation

<!-- Optional: derive the key result or provide a clear proof sketch. -->

Show the reasoning in enough detail that the reader can identify each assumption
and transition. Prefer one illuminating derivation over several routine ones.

## Result

$$
\boxed{\ldots}
$$

## Interpretation

Translate the mathematical result into plain scientific language.

# Estimation and Inference

<!-- Optional: use for estimators, tests, and fitted models. -->

## Target and estimator

Define the estimand before the estimator:

$$
\theta=\ldots,
\qquad
\hat{\theta}=\ldots
$$

## Sampling distribution and uncertainty

Explain repeated-sampling behavior, bias, variance, and standard error when relevant.

$$
\operatorname{SE}(\hat{\theta})=\ldots
$$

## Interval or test

Define the confidence interval, credible interval, test statistic, null distribution,
or decision rule that belongs to this topic. State what the result does and does
not mean, and report effect size alongside statistical significance where relevant.

# Bayesian Perspective

<!-- Optional: keep when Bayesian reasoning adds real value. -->

$$
p(\theta\mid D)\propto p(D\mid\theta)p(\theta)
$$

Explain the prior, likelihood, posterior, computation, and posterior predictive
checks. Compare frequentist and Bayesian interpretations only when that comparison
clarifies the topic.

# Worked Example

Use a dataset small enough to calculate or reason through manually. Show the
essential steps and interpret the final result in plain language.

```text
Data → calculation or model → uncertainty → scientific interpretation
```

# Bioinformatics Application

<!-- Optional for abstract foundations; strongly recommended for applied topics. -->

Describe the organism or biological system, experimental unit, sample size, data
type, outcome, covariates, and approximate scale. Separate statistical interpretation
from biological interpretation.

